```grf:context id="process-lifetime-and-children"
{
  "format": "grf.public-document/v1",
  "title": "Process lifetime and child commands â€” 0.6.1",
  "content": "Linux commands run under a dedicated subreaper. On normal completion, timeout, cancellation or death of the controlling Python process, the supervisor kills and reaps descendants, including double-forked children that start new sessions. It adopts only its own command's descendants, not unrelated children of the caller. Windows keeps the assigned job with kill-on-close and no breakaway. Other POSIX systems retain process-group cleanup; session escapes there are not covered. An uninterruptible kernel task or forcibly killed supervisor cannot be promised immediate cleanup.\n\nBy default, descendants share the initial command's approval. To prohibit child-process creation, add children: deny to a check object in project.grf, or to the action object in a downstream example document. Allowed values are inherit and deny. Omission preserves existing behavior. The policy is included in an explicitly configured action's admission hash and recorded in check receipts; changing it changes the action being approved. A legacy operator policy must approve the new exact action.\n\nThe Python API also accepts run_managed(..., allow_children=False). Windows uses an active-process job limit for the trusted broker plus requested root. Linux x86_64 and aarch64 use an inherited seccomp restriction on fork/vfork/non-thread clone; clone3 returns ENOSYS so libc can fall back to inspected clone. Ordinary threads remain allowed. Unsupported platforms or inability to install the requested restriction fail before the requested program starts.\n\nThis mode blocks new descendant processes, not every dangerous action. The approved program can still act in-process, replace itself with exec, or contact an already-running external service. It is not a filesystem/network sandbox, arbitrary-program allowlist, universal tool interceptor or per-descendant independent approval system.\n\nNo process mode rolls back writes or cancels work already delegated to external services. Checks that need subprocesses must use inherit and accept shared approval, or be decomposed into separately declared root checks. Never silently change existing projects' pinned runtimes or active sessions.\n\n\nVerified CPython Windows virtual-environment redirectors use their configured base interpreter with CPython virtual-environment identity, preserving argv, sys.prefix, sys.executable and site-packages without granting extra child-process slots. Unknown launchers receive no additional permission.\n"
}
